<Base title="Run a node" description="Run a khensu.ñü node on a Raspberry Pi 5, an old phone, or your computer in the background.">
  <p class="eyebrow">Run a node</p>
  <h1>Be part of the network</h1>
  <StatStrip
    items={[
      { n: 3400, label: 'About this many people a day on an old iPhone 15 Pro' },
      { n: 1700, label: 'About this many people a day on a Raspberry Pi 5' },
      { n: 50, unit: '%', label: 'Or less of a Mac’s idle processing power' },
      { n: 0, pre: '$', label: 'To start on a phone you already own' },
    ]}
  >
    Figures from the hardware table below: each node shares half its connection, the iPhone on 2 Gbit fibre over Wi-Fi 6E and the Pi on gigabit fibre.
  </StatStrip>
  <p class="lede">A connection and a power socket, nothing more. It earns for the work it can prove it did.</p>
  <p>It forwards traffic it can’t read and keeps encrypted mailboxes for people who are offline.</p>

  <h2>Hardware</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Old or unused phone</td><td>The best way to run a node, and the most profitable: you already own it, so there’s nothing to buy and what it earns is yours from day one. Leave it on Wi-Fi and plugged in, with khensu.ñü open in node mode. An iPhone 15 Pro on 2 Gbit fibre over Wi-Fi 6E, sharing half, carries about 3,400 people a day.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Your laptop or desktop</td><td>Runs a node in the background while you work, using 50% or less of its idle processing power, on a Mac.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="https://www.raspberrypi.com/products/raspberry-pi-5/">Raspberry Pi 5</a></td><td>A small, quiet computer that uses about as much power as a light bulb. On gigabit fibre, sharing half its connection, it carries about 1,700 people a day.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Connection</h2>
  <p>Fibre, cable, 5G home internet or satellite internet. A node on satellite internet is a full node. Where there’s no internet at all, nodes nearby reach each other directly.</p>

